(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endocrine-physiology-1-quiz"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology I: Pituitary & Thyroid — Quiz (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the three hormone classes is derived from cholesterol, is lipid soluble, and has very little storage?","choices":["Steroid hormones","Eicosanoid hormones","Protein and peptide hormones","Tyrosine-derived (amine) hormones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Steroid hormones are derived from cholesterol, are lipid soluble, and have very little storage (e.g., cortisol and aldosterone from the adrenal cortex, estrogen/progesterone from the ovaries, testosterone from the testes). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which two hormones are grouped under the tyrosine (amine)-derived class alongside thyroid hormones?","choices":["Insulin and glucagon","Estrogen and progesterone","Norepinephrine and epinephrine","Cortisol and aldosterone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine (adrenal medulla catecholamines) are tyrosine-derived, grouped with thyroid hormones (T3/T4) in the amine class. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Protein and peptide hormones, such as growth hormone, ACTH, and insulin, share which general property?","choices":["Derived from cholesterol","Bound exclusively to thyroxine-binding globulin","Water-soluble, stored in secretory vesicles","Lipid soluble, stored freely in the cytoplasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Protein and peptide hormones (3-200 amino acids) are water-soluble and stored in secretory vesicles. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which receptor location would a water-soluble peptide hormone most likely act on, given it cannot easily cross the lipid cell membrane?","choices":["Cell cytoplasm receptors","It has no receptor interaction","Cell membrane receptors","Cell nucleus receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Water-soluble hormones (like peptides) generally act on cell membrane receptors, since they cannot easily diffuse across the lipid bilayer to reach intracellular receptors. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the listed mechanisms of hormone clearance?","choices":["Binding to tissue receptors","Metabolic destruction (endocytosis, enzymes)","Active reabsorption by the thyroid gland","Excretion by the kidneys into urine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Active reabsorption by the thyroid gland is not one of the listed hormone clearance mechanisms. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Negative feedback, described as the most common hormone control mechanism, works by:","choices":["The biological effect suppressing further hormone release from the endocrine cell","Hormone levels rising and falling on a fixed daily clock, independent of biological effect","Hormones only being released once per lifetime","The biological effect further stimulating more hormone release, in an escalating loop"],"answer":0,"exp":"In negative feedback, the biological effect produced by the hormone feeds back to suppress further release from the endocrine cell — the most common control mechanism. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In the lecture's example of negative feedback, angiotensin II inhibits which upstream component of its own cascade?","choices":["Angiotensinogen production by the liver directly","Renin","Aldosterone secretion","ACE"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the negative-feedback example given, angiotensin II feeds back to inhibit renin. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Approximately how low is typical plasma hormone concentration, as emphasized in this lecture?","choices":["Milligram (10⁻³ g/mL) range","Picogram (10⁻¹² g/mL) range","Nanogram (10⁻⁹ g/mL) range","Microgram (10⁻⁶ g/mL) range"],"answer":1,"exp":"Plasma hormone concentration is emphasized as extremely low — on the order of a picogram (0.000000000001 g/mL blood). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell type produces growth hormone (somatotropin)?","choices":["Thyrotropes","Corticotropes","Lactotropes","Somatotropes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Somatotropes produce growth hormone (GH, or somatotropin). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell type produces adrenocorticotropin (ACTH)?","choices":["Lactotropes","Corticotropes","Somatotropes","Gonadotropes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Corticotropes produce ACTH (adrenocorticotropin/corticotropin). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell type produces both follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone?","choices":["Gonadotropes","Somatotropes","Corticotropes","Thyrotropes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Gonadotropes produce both FSH and LH. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic hormone stimulates GH secretion by somatotropes?","choices":["Prolactin-inhibiting hormone (dopamine)","Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH)","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone","Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) is the hypothalamic peptide that stimulates GH secretion by somatotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What effect does dopamine, functioning as prolactin-inhibiting hormone, have on lactotropes?","choices":["Stimulates prolactin secretion","Converts lactotropes into somatotropes","Inhibits prolactin secretion","Has no effect on lactotropes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Dopamine, acting as prolactin-inhibiting hormone, inhibits prolactin secretion by lactotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of the anterior pituitary is composed of somatotropes?","choices":["About 65%","About 35%","About 95%","About 5%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Somatotropes make up about 35% of the anterior pituitary. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Where does growth hormone primarily act, per this lecture?","choices":["The kidney and adrenal cortex exclusively","The pancreas and spleen exclusively","The liver and peripheral tissues","The thyroid gland exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Growth hormone acts on the liver and peripheral tissues. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the chemical structure of growth hormone, as described in this lecture?","choices":["A steroid derived from cholesterol","A gas-phase signaling molecule","A tyrosine derivative bound to thyroxine-binding globulin","A protein hormone of 191 amino acids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Growth hormone is a protein hormone composed of 191 amino acids. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Growth hormone's three broad metabolic actions include increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and:","choices":["Decreased carbohydrate utilization","Decreased protein synthesis","No effect on carbohydrate metabolism","Increased carbohydrate utilization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Growth hormone's three broad metabolic actions are increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and DEcreased carbohydrate utilization. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does growth hormone inhibit carbohydrate utilization?","choices":["By decreasing glucose uptake in muscle and fat, and increasing hepatic gluconeogenesis","By directly destroying circulating glucose molecules through oxidative enzymatic breakdown","By stimulating pancreatic insulin release exclusively, with no direct hepatic effect","By increasing glucose uptake in both muscle and fat, and lowering hepatic output"],"answer":0,"exp":"GH decreases glucose uptake in both muscle and fat, while increasing hepatic gluconeogenesis — together reducing carbohydrate utilization. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In GH-driven bone growth, chondrocyte precursor cells are converted into which cell type that GH also stimulates?","choices":["Adipocytes","Myocytes","Osteoclasts","Osteoblasts"],"answer":3,"exp":"GH increases conversion of chondrocytic cells to osteogenic cells and stimulates osteoblasts, driving bone growth. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"The 'somatomedin hypothesis' relates growth hormone's growth-promoting effects to which mediator?","choices":["Cortisol, a glucocorticoid stress hormone","Insulin, acting directly on its own receptor","Thyroid hormone (T3/T4) acting directly","IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The somatomedin hypothesis proposes that many of GH's growth-promoting effects are mediated through IGF-1. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter class is described as increasing GH secretion via hypothalamic control?","choices":["GABA","Catecholamines","Acetylcholine exclusively","Serotonin exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Catecholamines are described as increasing GH secretion as part of the hypothalamic control pathway (via the ventromedial nucleus/GHRH). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Average growth hormone concentration changes across the lifespan. Which age range has the LOWEST average concentration, and what value goes with it?","choices":["40–70 years, about 1.6 ng/mL","20–40 years, about 3 ng/mL","5–20 years, about 6 ng/mL","Growth hormone concentration does not vary with age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Growth hormone falls with age: about 6 ng/mL at 5–20 years, about 3 ng/mL at 20–40 years, and about 1.6 ng/mL at 40–70 years. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"The hypothalamic ventromedial nucleus contributes to GH regulation primarily by producing:","choices":["CRH","TRH","GHRH","GnRH"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ventromedial nucleus is associated with GHRH (growth hormone-releasing hormone) production in the hypothalamic control of GH. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Posterior pituitary hormones are synthesized in the hypothalamus and transported to nerve terminals via which carrier molecules?","choices":["Albumin","Neurophysins","Thyroxine-binding globulins","Transcortin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Posterior pituitary hormones (ADH, oxytocin) are synthesized in the hypothalamus and transported to nerve terminals via neurophysins. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Where do the nerve fibers carrying posterior pituitary hormones terminate?","choices":["At the thyroid gland directly","At the adrenal medulla","At the anterior pituitary's portal vessels","At capillaries in the neurohypophysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nerve fibers from the hypothalamus terminate at capillaries in the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary), where hormones are released into the blood. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"ADH (antidiuretic hormone/vasopressin) is produced primarily in which hypothalamic nuclei?","choices":["Supraoptic nuclei (about 80–90%)","Arcuate nuclei","Paraventricular nuclei exclusively","Ventromedial nuclei"],"answer":0,"exp":"ADH is produced primarily (about 80–90%) in the supraoptic nuclei of the hypothalamus. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Structurally, ADH is best classified as:","choices":["A large 191-amino-acid protein","A peptide hormone of 9 amino acids","A cholesterol-derived steroid hormone","A tyrosine-derived catecholamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"ADH (AVP) is a peptide hormone of 9 amino acids, water soluble. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why does T3 act faster than T4 despite T4 being secreted in greater quantity?","choices":["T4 cannot bind the receptor at all","T3 is not bound to any plasma protein while T4 is fully protein-bound, explaining all the speed difference","T3 has a higher affinity for the thyroid hormone nuclear receptor and a shorter latency","T3 has a longer half-life than T4"],"answer":2,"exp":"T3 has a higher affinity for the thyroid hormone receptor than T4 and a shorter latency (6–12 hours) compared to T4's 2–3 day latency. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"High levels of T3/T4 are associated with which change in basal metabolic rate and thermogenesis?","choices":["Both decrease","BMR increases while thermogenesis decreases","Both increase","Neither changes"],"answer":2,"exp":"High T3/T4 levels increase both basal metabolic rate and thermogenesis. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is thyroid hormone's effect on serum cholesterol at high (versus low) T3/T4 levels?","choices":["Thyroid hormone has no effect on serum cholesterol","Both high and low T3/T4 increase serum cholesterol equally","High T3/T4 increases serum cholesterol; low T3/T4 decreases it","High T3/T4 decreases serum cholesterol; low T3/T4 increases it"],"answer":3,"exp":"High T3/T4 is associated with increased lipolysis and DEcreased serum cholesterol, while low T3/T4 is associated with decreased lipolysis and increased serum cholesterol. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 72"}]};
